  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 AM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nThe weather pattern across eastern Idaho will remain generally \nbenign through the workweek, characterized by an absence of \nhigh-impact systems and continued above-normal temperatures. \nEarly morning satellite imagery shows mid-to-high level \ncloudiness streaming over the region, but tranquil weather will \npersist beneath high pressure anchored over the Great Basin. \nStrong atmospheric subsidence and rising 500 mb heights will \npromote unseasonably warm afternoons, with daytime highs running\n7 to 12 degrees above seasonal averages. Lower-elevation \nvalleys across the Snake River Plain and Magic Valley will \ncomfortably reach the upper 50s to lower 60s under light, \nterrain-driven winds, providing very pleasant late-autumn \nconditions.\n\nThe tranquil synoptic pattern begins to break down heading into\nthe weekend as an upper-level trough over the Gulf of Alaska \ntaps into Pacific moisture and ejects shortwave energy across \nthe Pacific Northwest. An initial wave of moisture and an \nassociated surface cold front will move into eastern Idaho late \nFriday night and persist through much of Saturday. Thickening \ncloud cover and precipitation will suppress daytime heating, \nholding Saturday high temperatures mainly in the 40s across the \nregion.\n\nThe second half of the weekend offers a brief lull between \nsystems. Eastern Idaho will reside in between moisture surges on\nSunday, allowing precipitation to taper off and keeping \nconditions predominantly dry. Afternoon temperatures will \nrebound slightly under partly cloudy skies, running a touch \nwarmer than Saturday.\n\nA much more pronounced and prolonged pattern shift takes hold to\nkick off next week. Global deterministic guidance and ensemble\nclusters remain in strong agreement regarding broad upper-level\ntroughing anchoring over the western United States. This setup\nwill usher in an expansive cold air mass, making below-normal\ntemperatures increasingly likely along with daily chances for\nscattered rain and mountain snow across eastern Idaho. Depending\non the depth of cold air advection, snow levels could reach\nvalley floors toward the tail end of the forecast period,\nparticularly during overnight and early morning hours. While\nconsiderable ensemble spread remains regarding specific\nprecipitation amounts and timing this far out, confidence is\nhigh in a transition to colder, unsettled weather.",
